        <div id="airline-rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. TSA and Airline Rules: The 100Wh Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every power bank question for travel starts with one number: 100 watt-hours. Under FAA rules, and the matching ICAO rules most of the world follows, lithium-ion batteries up to 100Wh can go in your carry-on with no airline approval. Banks from 101 to 160Wh need airline approval, and you are limited to two of them. Anything over 160Wh cannot fly on a passenger plane at all, whether in the cabin or checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second rule matters just as much: spare lithium batteries, including every power bank, must travel in the cabin. They are banned from checked luggage because a cell fire in the hold cannot be reached by the crew. If an agent gate-checks your carry-on, take the power bank out first. That is the single most common way travelers end up separated from their bank or having it confiscated.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter at Security</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Carry-on limit, no approval</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">160Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Absolute max, airline approval</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">27,000mAh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Roughly 100Wh at 3.7V</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Power banks allowed in checked bags</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Most banks list capacity in mAh, not Wh, so you need to convert. Multiply the mAh rating by the cell voltage, usually 3.6 or 3.7V, and divide by 1,000. A 10,000mAh bank is about 37Wh, 20,000mAh is about 74Wh, and 26,800mAh is right at 99Wh. That is why you will never see a mainstream travel bank much above 27,000mAh. The Wh figure should also be printed on the case, and security staff in some countries will reject a bank whose label is worn off or missing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rules got stricter in 2025</h3>

          <p>
            After a run of in-cabin battery fires, several airlines tightened their own policies beyond the regulatory minimum. Southwest now requires power banks to stay visible, not buried in a bag, while in use. Emirates and a number of Asian carriers now ban using or charging power banks during the flight, and some limit passengers to one bank. Since June 2025, mainland China has refused power banks without a 3C certification mark on domestic flights. Check your specific airline before you fly, especially on international itineraries, and expect rules to keep tightening rather than loosening.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Capacity You Actually Need
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rated mAh vs Usable Charge</h3>

          <p>
            The rated mAh is not what reaches your phone. Converting the cell voltage up to USB voltage, plus heat and cable losses, eats roughly 30 to 40 percent, so a 10,000mAh bank delivers something like 6,000 to 7,000mAh of usable charge. Modern flagship phones carry batteries of roughly 13 to 19Wh, which is why a 10,000mAh bank gives about two full charges of a current iPhone or Galaxy, not the three or four the box implies.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match Capacity to Time Between Outlets</h3>

          <p>
            Match capacity to how long you go between outlets. For a day of sightseeing or a long travel day, 5,000 to 10,000mAh is plenty and keeps weight under about 250g. For a long-haul flight plus a day of connections, or for sharing among two people, 20,000mAh is the sweet spot. Only go to 25,000mAh and above if you are charging a laptop, a tablet and phones, or you are going somewhere outlets are rare.
          </p>

