        <div id="usb-c-pd" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. USB-C PD and Charging Speed
          </h2>

          <p>
            USB-C Power Delivery, or PD, is the standard that lets a bank and a device negotiate a higher voltage for faster charging. Every phone sold today, including every iPhone since the iPhone 15, charges over USB-C PD, so a bank with a PD port charges an iPhone, a Pixel or a Galaxy at close to wall-charger speed. Avoid banks that only offer USB-A ports; they are cheaper for a reason and charge modern phones slowly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The wattage you need depends on what you carry. Phones top out around 20 to 45W, so a 20W or 30W port is ideal and anything higher is wasted on a phone. Tablets like an iPad Pro charge faster with 30 to 45W. Laptops need 45W at a minimum for a small ultrabook and 65 to 140W for larger machines; our <a href="/best-laptop-power-bank/">laptop power bank guide</a> covers that math in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the total output figure, not just the per-port rating. Many banks advertise a high single-port wattage but share a lower total across all ports. A bank rated 30W might drop to 24W total when you connect two devices, so neither charges at full speed. If two people regularly charge from one bank, look at the combined rating on the spec sheet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pass-through and recharge speed</h3>

          <p>
            Pass-through charging means the bank can charge your phone while it is itself plugged into the wall, so one outlet in a hotel room or airport lounge tops up both overnight. Recharge speed also matters more than people expect: a 20,000mAh bank that only accepts 18W input takes five or more hours to refill, which is useless on a 90-minute layover. Banks that accept 30W or more refill in roughly two to three hours, and flagship banks with 100W-plus input refill in about an hour.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="magsafe-qi2" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. MagSafe, Qi2 and Wireless Banks
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Magnetic Banks and Qi2</h3>

          <p>
            Magnetic banks snap onto the back of an iPhone and charge without a cable, which is the most convenient way to use a bank while walking, navigating or standing in a queue. Qi2 is the open standard built on Apple's MagSafe, and a certified Qi2 bank charges iPhone 12 and later models at up to 15W. The newer Qi2.2 spec raises that to 25W on recent iPhones and compatible Android phones, and those banks are starting to appear in 2025 and 2026.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip Non-Certified Magnetic Banks</h3>

          <p>
            Avoid non-certified magnetic banks that just say "magnetic wireless" or "MagSafe compatible" without Qi2. Most charge at 7.5W or less, misalign easily, and run hot. The Qi2 logo on the box is the simplest filter in this whole category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wireless Costs Capacity</h3>

          <p>
            Wireless costs you capacity. Induction loses more energy as heat than a cable does, so a 10,000mAh magnetic bank typically delivers about one and a half phone charges instead of two. Phones also slow wireless charging when they get warm, which happens fast in a hot car or in summer sun. Every good magnetic bank has a USB-C port too, so plug in by cable when speed matters and use the magnet when convenience does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Magnetic Charging on Android</h3>

          <p>
            For Android users, magnetic charging works on phones that support Qi2 natively and on most others with a magnetic ring case. If your phone does not support Qi2 at all, skip the magnetic premium and buy a cabled bank with a built-in cable, which gives similar grab-and-go convenience at lower weight and more usable capacity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="built-in-cables" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Built-In Cables vs Separate Cables
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why a Built-In Cable Helps</h3>

          <p>
            The most common travel failure is not a dead bank, it is a missing cable. A bank with a built-in USB-C cable removes that problem entirely, and on the better designs like the Anker Nano that same cable also recharges the bank, so you only need a wall charger. For phone-only travelers this is the single feature most worth paying for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Durability Tradeoff</h3>

          <p>
            The tradeoff is durability. A built-in cable is a single point of failure, and if it frays at the joint the whole bank is compromised, whereas a separate cable costs a few dollars to replace. Look for designs that tuck the cable into a channel or retract it, and avoid wrapping it tightly. Owners commonly report built-in cables lasting years when they are not used as a handle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lightning Devices</h3>

          <p>
            If your devices still use Lightning, such as an older iPhone or AirPods case, either buy a bank with a USB-C port and a short USB-C to Lightning cable, or accept that a bank with a built-in Lightning cable will be obsolete with your next phone. USB-C is the safe long-term bet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choosing a Separate Cable</h3>

          <p>
            For separate cables, carry one good USB-C to USB-C cable rated for 100W or 240W. It charges every phone, tablet and laptop, and a short 1-foot length is easier to manage on a tray table than a 6-foot cable. Label it or use a bright color so you do not leave it plugged into a seat-back port.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="international" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. International Travel Tips
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Voltage and Wall Chargers Abroad</h3>

          <p>
            A power bank itself does not care what country you are in. It runs on its own battery, and the concern is the wall charger you use to refill it. Almost every modern USB-C wall charger, including GaN chargers, is rated 100 to 240V, so it works worldwide with only a plug adapter. Check the fine print on the charger, not the bank, and never use a cheap voltage converter with a USB charger that is already universal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pair the Bank With a GaN Charger</h3>

          <p>
            A compact GaN wall charger with two or three USB-C ports is the best companion for a travel bank. One 65W charger can refill the bank, your phone and a laptop overnight from a single outlet, which matters in older hotels with one usable socket. Pair it with a slim plug adapter for your destination rather than a bulky all-in-one adapter with weak USB ports built in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Certification Marks Abroad</h3>

          <p>
            Certification marks matter abroad. China requires a 3C mark on power banks for domestic flights, and security in several Asian countries has become stricter about legible capacity labels. Buy from established brands like Anker, UGREEN, Nitecore or Jackery that print watt-hours clearly and carry common certifications, and keep the box or a screenshot of the spec page on your phone in case you are questioned.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carry Two Banks for Redundancy</h3>

          <p>
            For longer trips, think about redundancy. A single bank that gets confiscated, lost or fails leaves you stranded, so many frequent travelers carry two 10,000mAh banks rather than one 20,000mAh bank. The total weight is similar, both fly easily, and one can charge in the hotel while you carry the other.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety: Heat, Swelling and Cheap Banks
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Avoid Cheap No-Name Banks</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium-ion power banks are safe when built properly and treated reasonably, but cheap no-name banks are where most incidents start. Inflated capacity claims are a red flag: a slim, light bank claiming 50,000mAh is almost certainly lying, and a bank that lies about capacity may also cut corners on cell quality and protection circuits. We recommend only established brands for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Retire a Bank</h3>

          <p>
            Retire any bank that swells, smells of solvent, gets unusually hot during normal charging, or has a cracked case. A swollen bank should not be charged again and should not be flown. Take it to a battery recycling point; most electronics stores accept them. Do not throw it in household trash, where it can start a garbage truck or landfill fire.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Away From Heat</h3>

          <p>
            Heat is the enemy of battery life. Do not leave a bank on a car dashboard, in direct sun on a beach or under a pillow while charging. On a plane, keep it in the seat pocket or on the tray where you can see it, both because some airlines now require it and because you will notice early if something goes wrong. If a bank smokes in the cabin, alert the crew immediately rather than trying to handle it yourself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storing Banks Between Trips</h3>

          <p>
            For storage between trips, keep banks at about 40 to 60 percent and top them up every few months. Leaving a bank at 100 percent for months, or fully drained, shortens its life. The same guidance applies to the banks in your <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">power outage kit</a>, and if you keep one bank for both jobs, recharge it as soon as you get home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="road-trips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Road Trips, Camping and When to Go Bigger
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Driving Changes the Math</h3>

          <p>
            Driving removes the airline limits, and that changes the math. In a car you can carry a much larger battery, and a small power station with AC outlets will charge laptops, cameras and drones, run a 12V cooler and power a CPAP at a campsite. Our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> covers those units, and the <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion comparison</a> explains why longer-lived chemistry is worth it for gear you use every weekend.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Jackery Fits</h3>

          <p>
            The Jackery Explorer 100 Plus on our list is a middle ground: LiFePO4 durability and a car outlet, but under 100Wh so it still flies. Above that size you are buying a station that stays home or in the car. A 250 to 300Wh unit like the EcoFlow River 3 is about the largest that is still easy to carry, and you can keep it charged from a <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">folding solar panel</a> for multi-day trips.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Larger Stations Cannot Fly</h3>

          <p>
            Remember that larger stations cannot fly. A 245Wh or larger unit is over the 160Wh absolute limit, so it is not a matter of airline approval, it simply cannot go on a passenger plane. If your trip combines flying and camping, fly with a 90 to 99Wh bank and rent or buy anything larger at the destination.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Simple Setup for Most Travelers</h3>

          <p>
            For most travelers, though, the right setup is simple: a 10,000mAh bank for daily carry, a 20,000 to 25,000mAh bank if you carry a laptop or tablet, and a multi-port GaN wall charger to refill everything overnight. That covers almost every trip from a weekend city break to a two-week international itinerary.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I bring a power bank on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in your carry-on only. Power banks up to 100Wh, which covers nearly every bank up to about 27,000mAh, are allowed without airline approval. Banks from 101 to 160Wh need airline approval and you can carry two at most. Anything over 160Wh is prohibited, and no power bank may go in checked luggage on any airline.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best power bank size for travel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most travelers, 10,000mAh is the right size. It weighs about 150 to 250g, gives roughly two full charges of a modern flagship phone, and is far below every airline limit. Step up to 20,000 to 25,000mAh if you also need to charge a tablet or laptop, or if two people share one bank on long trips.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many mAh is 100Wh?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the typical 3.6 to 3.7V cell voltage, 100Wh is about 27,000mAh. Multiply mAh by voltage and divide by 1,000 to get watt-hours. A 20,000mAh bank is about 74Wh and a 26,250mAh bank is about 99.75Wh. The Wh figure should be printed on the case, and security staff check that label, not the mAh.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a MagSafe or Qi2 power bank worth it for iPhone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If convenience matters more than capacity, yes. A Qi2 bank snaps on and charges an iPhone 12 or later at up to 15W without a cable, which is great while walking or navigating. The cost is efficiency: a 10,000mAh magnetic bank usually delivers about one and a half charges rather than two. Avoid uncertified magnetic banks; look for the Qi2 logo.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use my power bank during the flight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the airline. FAA rules allow it, but some carriers have restricted in-flight use since 2025. Southwest requires banks to stay visible while in use, and Emirates and several Asian airlines ban using or charging power banks onboard entirely. Check your airline's current policy before you fly, and keep the bank where you can see it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a voltage converter for my power bank abroad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The bank runs on its own battery, and nearly every modern USB-C wall charger is rated 100 to 240V, so it works worldwide with only a plug adapter. Check the input rating printed on your wall charger to confirm. Never plug a universal USB charger into a voltage converter; it is unnecessary and can damage the charger.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a power bank hold its charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A quality lithium-ion bank loses only a few percent per month in storage, so a full bank is still useful after several months in a drawer. For best battery life, store it around 40 to 60 percent between trips and top it up every few months. Most banks last a few hundred to about 1,000 charge cycles before capacity noticeably drops.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Packing the bank in checked luggage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Power banks are banned from checked bags everywhere, and an agent who finds one will remove it or hold the bag. The bigger risk is a last-minute gate check of your carry-on. Always pull the bank and any spare batteries out before handing over a bag, even for a short regional flight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting huge mAh claims from unknown brands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thin, cheap bank claiming 30,000 or 50,000mAh is almost always exaggerated, and a bank that misstates capacity may also skimp on protection circuits. Stick to established brands that print the watt-hour rating clearly, and be suspicious of any listing that does not state Wh at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a USB-A-only bank in 2026</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old-style banks with only USB-A ports charge modern phones slowly and cannot charge laptops at all. Every current iPhone and Android phone charges fastest over USB-C PD. Pay the few extra dollars for a USB-C PD port of at least 20W; it is the feature that makes a bank actually useful.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the cable or the recharge plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full bank is useless without the right cable, and an empty bank is useless without a way to refill it. Either buy a bank with a built-in cable or keep a dedicated cable in its pouch, and pack a USB-C wall charger rated 100 to 240V so you can recharge abroad.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying more capacity than you will carry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 25,000mAh bank is impressive on paper and annoying in a day bag. If it ends up left in the hotel because it is heavy, it is not helping. Most phone-only travelers are better served by a 10,000mAh bank they always carry than a giant one they leave behind.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

